Understanding Kubernetes Architecture — From Control Plane to Worker Nodes

One of the most important things I’ve learned while working with Kubernetes is that understanding the architecture makes troubleshooting much easier.

At a high level, a Kubernetes cluster has two major parts:

🔹 Control Plane (Master Node)
This is the brain of the Kubernetes cluster. It includes components like:

• API Server – The entry point for communicating with the cluster
• Scheduler – Decides which worker node should run a Pod
• etcd – Stores the cluster's configuration and state
• Controllers – Continuously monitor and maintain the desired state
• Cloud Controller Manager – Integrates Kubernetes with cloud infrastructure

🔹 Worker Nodes
These are the machines where our applications actually run.

Key components include:

• Kubelet – Communicates with the API Server and manages Pods
• Container Runtime – Runs containers
• Kube-proxy – Helps with networking and service communication

The simple way I remember it:

👉 Control Plane = Decides what should happen
👉 Worker Node = Makes it happen

Understanding this flow helped me connect the dots between Pods, Nodes, Scheduling, Networking, and Container Runtime instead of memorizing Kubernetes components individually.
